.TH LIBMU2EPROBE 3 "2026-09-17" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0" "Mu2e DAQ Operations"
.SH NAME
libmu2eprobe \- parallel TCP reachability probing for the Mu2e DAQ recovery tools
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <mu2eprobe/probe.hpp>   /* C++ */
.B #include <mu2eprobe/probe.h>     /* C   */
.PP
.B cc ... \-lmu2eprobe
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B libmu2eprobe
answers one question quickly: of these hosts, which are up? Phase 1 of a
recovery has to answer it for about sixty hosts on three network segments
before it can do anything else, and phase 3 probes a full mesh on the data
network.
.PP
The probe is a non-blocking TCP connect with a bounded
.BR poll (2),
not ICMP. It needs no privilege, it tests something more useful than bare IP
reachability, and TCP/22 is not filtered on the lab network. A
.I refused
connection counts as reachable: the host answered, its sshd simply is not
listening yet, which during a power-on is the normal intermediate state.
.PP
The library is C++17 with a C ABI wrapper and pybind11 bindings. It has no
dependencies beyond the standard library and POSIX sockets, and OpenMP if it is
available \(em without OpenMP the sweep is correct but serial.
.SH C++ INTERFACE
.SS Types
.TP
.B enum class mu2eprobe::Outcome
.BR Open ", " Refused ", " Timeout ", " Unresolved ", " Error .
.TP
.B struct mu2eprobe::Result
.RS
.nf
std::string host;        /* host as given                       */
std::string address;     /* resolved address, empty if unresolved */
std::uint16_t port;
Outcome outcome;
double elapsed_ms;
std::string detail;      /* errno text for Outcome::Error       */
bool reachable() const;  /* Open or Refused                     */
const char* outcome_name() const;
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B struct mu2eprobe::Options
.RS
.nf
std::uint16_t port = 22;
int timeout_ms = 2000;
int threads = 0;         /* 0 = the OpenMP default              */
bool resolve_only = false;
.fi
.RE
.SS Functions
.TP
.BI "Result mu2eprobe::probe_one(const std::string& " host ", const Options& " options );
Probe one host. Never throws; failures are reported in the
.BR Result .
.TP
.BI "std::vector<Result> mu2eprobe::probe_many(const std::vector<std::string>& " hosts ", const Options& " options );
Probe many hosts in parallel. Results are returned
.I in input order
regardless of completion order, because the caller's node list is ordered
meaningfully and should not have to be re-sorted.
.TP
.BI "std::vector<std::string> mu2eprobe::reachable_hosts(const std::vector<Result>& " results );
The hosts that answered, in input order.
.TP
.B const char* mu2eprobe::version();
.TQ
.B bool mu2eprobe::has_openmp();
Library version, and whether parallelism is actually compiled in.
.SH C INTERFACE
.nf
.BI "void mu2e_probe_default_options(mu2e_probe_options_t* " options );
.BI "int  mu2e_probe_one(const char* " host ", const mu2e_probe_options_t* " options ,
.BI "                    mu2e_probe_result_t* " out );
.BI "int  mu2e_probe_many(const char* const* " hosts ", size_t " count ,
.BI "                     const mu2e_probe_options_t* " options ,
.BI "                     mu2e_probe_result_t* " results );
.BI "const char* mu2e_probe_outcome_name(int " outcome );
.BI "const char* mu2e_probe_version(void);"
.BI "int  mu2e_probe_has_openmp(void);"
.fi
.PP
.B mu2e_probe_one
returns the outcome code
.RB ( MU2E_PROBE_OPEN ", " MU2E_PROBE_REFUSED ", " MU2E_PROBE_TIMEOUT ,
.BR MU2E_PROBE_UNRESOLVED ", " MU2E_PROBE_ERROR ).
.B mu2e_probe_many
returns the number of hosts that answered, or \-1 on a bad argument; the caller
supplies the results array, and the strings inside each result are fixed-size
buffers, so nothing is ever freed by the caller.
.PP
All functions are safe to call from multiple threads.
.B mu2e_probe_many
runs its own parallelism internally.
.SH PYTHON INTERFACE
When the pybind11 extension is built, the same sweep is available as:
.RS
.EX
import mu2eprobe
results = mu2eprobe.probe_many(hosts, port=22, timeout_ms=2000)
up = [r.host for r in results if r.reachable]
mu2eprobe.has_openmp()
.EE
.RE
.PP
The GIL is released for the duration of a sweep. The recovery tools do not
import this directly \(em they use
.I mu2edaq_power_recovery.sweep,
which uses the extension when it is present and a thread-pool implementation
with identical semantics when it is not.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.EX
#include <mu2eprobe/probe.hpp>
#include <cstdio>

int main() {
  mu2eprobe::Options options;
  options.timeout_ms = 1000;
  const auto results = mu2eprobe::probe_many(
      {"mu2e-trk-01.fnal.gov", "mu2e-trk-02.fnal.gov"}, options);
  for (const auto& r : results)
    std::printf("%-30s %s\\n", r.host.c_str(), r.outcome_name());
}
.EE
.RE
.SH BUILDING
.RS
.EX
cmake \-S . \-B build \-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
cmake \-\-build build
ctest \-\-test\-dir build \-\-output\-on\-failure
cmake \-\-install build \-\-prefix /usr/local
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B \-DBUILD_BINDINGS=OFF
skips the Python extension;
.B \-DBUILD_PROBE=OFF
skips the library entirely.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2e-probe (1),
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1),
.BR connect (2),
.BR poll (2),
.BR getaddrinfo (3)
